 # Gaussian Voxel Kernel Functions (GVKF)
-
+Also called: GVKF (Gaussian Voxel Kernel Functions)
 
 ## Overview
-Gaussian Voxel Kernel Functions (GVKF) is a method for efficient 3D surface reconstruction in open scenes that establishes a continuous scene representation based on discrete 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) through kernel regression [S1]. It integrates fast 3DGS rasterization with implicit scene representations to achieve high-fidelity surface reconstruction while significantly reducing memory consumption compared to explicit discrete summation [S1]. Unlike standard 3DGS, which suffers from rough surface details in sparse areas and high memory usage, GVKF replaces discrete opacity values with Gaussian kernel functions decoded from a shared feature vector, enabling direct mesh extraction and better geometric continuity [S1].
+Gaussian Voxel Kernel Functions (GVKF) is a method for efficient and effective 3D surface reconstruction in open scenes that establishes a continuous scene representation based on discrete 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) through kernel regression [S1]. It integrates fast 3DGS rasterization with implicit scene representations to achieve high-fidelity open scene surface reconstruction, real-time rendering speed, and significant savings in storage and training memory consumption [S1]. Unlike standard 3DGS which uses explicit discrete summation leading to high memory consumption and rough surfaces in sparse areas, GVKF replaces discrete opacity values with Gaussian kernel functions, enabling direct mesh extraction and better 3D continuous surface fitting [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-GVKF manages Gaussian primitives via a spatial sparse voxel grid, decoding attributes from a compact feature vector using Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs) rather than storing explicit tensors for every primitive [S1]. This approach allows for dynamic generation and recycling of Gaussians, reducing memory overhead while maintaining expressive power [S1].
+**Inputs and Outputs**
+*   **Inputs:** Downsampled Structure-from-Motion (SfM) point clouds for initialization; camera parameters (position, orientation, intrinsics); feature vectors $F$ stored in voxels.
+*   **Outputs:** A continuous scene opacity density field; a reconstructed 3D mesh derived from the opacity field.
 
